# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

figdown 0.1 bitfield
# GRE header — transcribed from the ASCII diagram in RFC 2890 §2
# ("The proposed GRE header will have the following format", September 2000),
# which is RFC 2784 §2.1's base header plus the K and S bits and the two
# fields they gate. https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc2890.txt
#
# What this figure probes: fields whose PRESENCE depends on a flag bit
# elsewhere in the same header — Checksum and Reserved1 iff C = 1,
# Key iff K = 1, Sequence Number iff S = 1.
#
# Fidelity rule for this file: it carries EXACTLY the fields RFC 2890 §2
# draws. The payload packet follows on the wire (RFC 2784 §2 draws the
# whole encapsulation) but is outside this diagram, so it is not a field.
title "GRE header"

# Two carriers, two different jobs.
#
#   present="C = 1"  states THIS field's presence condition. It draws — a
#     caption under the block — so the human sees what the reading agent
#     sees. The VALUE is opaque prose: §12.7 lets an agent quote it and
#     forbids parsing it, evaluating it, or resolving `C` against the
#     `field "C"` declared below (the language has no reference grammar,
#     and a field name is a label, not an id — see `ANNOTATION-LOCATOR-SPLIT`).
#
#   class=ifc        states the CO-DEPENDENCY. Core §12.7 licenses a reading
#     agent to take a class reference plus that class's meaning as a
#     CATEGORY, so two fields carrying `class=ifc` are structurally in one
#     category and a reader may conclude they appear and disappear together.
#     That is real, machine-readable co-dependency, and it is the sanctioned
#     answer for a condition SHARED by several fields (genres/bitfield.md).
#
# Neither carrier resolves a name. Both are here because they say different
# things, and the descriptions below no longer repeat the condition a third
# time.
class ifc "Present only if the C (Checksum Present) bit is 1 — RFC 2784 §2.2"
class ifk "Present only if the K (Key Present) bit is 1 — RFC 2890 §2"
class ifs "Present only if the S (Sequence Number Present) bit is 1 — RFC 2890 §2"

# msb0: bit 0 is the MSB, ruler 0…31 left to right, so the bit numbers the
# RFC's own section headings use ("Checksum Present (bit 0)", "Reserved0
# (bits 1-12)", "Version Number (bits 13-15)") read straight off the ruler.
bitfield gre "GRE Header (RFC 2890 §2)" word=32 numbering=msb0

# Word 0 — 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 + 9 + 3 + 16 = 32 bits. The four flag bits are
# declared one per line so each gets its own bit position and its own note.
field "C" 1 description="Checksum Present (bit 0). If 1, the Checksum and Reserved1 fields are present and Checksum is valid — RFC 2784 §2.2"
# Bit 1 is drawn as an EMPTY cell in RFC 2890's figure and the RFC gives it
# no name there, so this file gives it none either: `""` records an empty
# label rather than inventing a caption. RFC 2784 §2.3 counts this bit
# inside Reserved0 (bits 1-12); RFC 1701 used it as the Routing Present bit.
field "" 1 description="bit 1: drawn blank in RFC 2890 §2; inside Reserved0 (bits 1-12) per RFC 2784 §2.3; the Routing Present bit in RFC 1701"
field "K" 1 description="Key Present (bit 2). If 1, the Key field is present — RFC 2890 §2"
field "S" 1 description="Sequence Number Present (bit 3). If 1, the Sequence Number field is present — RFC 2890 §2"
field "Reserved0" 9 description="bits 4-12. A receiver MUST discard a packet with any of bits 1-5 non-zero unless it implements RFC 1701; bits 6-12 MUST be sent as zero and ignored on receipt"
field "Ver" 3 description="Version Number (bits 13-15) MUST be zero — RFC 2784 §2.3.1"
field "Protocol Type" 16 fill=#dbeafe description="EtherType of the payload packet (RFC 1700 / IANA ETHER TYPES); 0x0800 when the payload is IPv4"

# Word 1 — present as a pair, or not at all. `present=` is the language's
# "present only if": the WIDTH is fixed and only the presence is not. A
# reader computing offsets across a conditionally present field must branch
# and emit both offset sets, one with it present and one without — the fields
# below each move 32 bits earlier when C = 0. The renderer dashes the cell
# and, because a condition is STATED here, draws it as a caption under the
# block, so the human sees exactly what the reading agent sees.
field "Checksum" 16 present="C = 1" class=ifc description="one's-complement checksum over the GRE header and the payload packet — RFC 2784 §2.5"
field "Reserved1" 16 present="C = 1" class=ifc description="MUST be transmitted as zero; present exactly when the Checksum field is present — RFC 2784 §2.6"

# Word 2, then word 3. Independent of each other and of word 1: with C=0,
# K=1, S=0 the Key is the SECOND word of the header, not the third.
field "Key" 32 present="K = 1" class=ifk fill=#fef9c3 description="four-octet flow identifier inserted by the encapsulator — RFC 2890 §2.1"
field "Sequence Number" 32 present="S = 1" class=ifs description="four-octet counter for in-order delivery, starting at 0 — RFC 2890 §2.2"

# Eight presence combinations (C, K, S independent) share this one drawing;
# the diagram above is the all-present case, which is what RFC 2890 draws.
# The header is 4 octets when C=K=S=0 and 16 octets when all three are set.
